    published: "2026-11-17",
    sections: [{ heading: "Lifestyle matters, but shame does not help", paragraphs: ["General health and fertility are connected, so it is fair to say that lifestyle can play a role. But the way this is often discussed, with blame and pressure, helps no one and can make people avoid care altogether. The honest message is gentler: some changes are worth making, they are not guaranteed fixes, and they are not a verdict on your worth.", "Both partners' health can matter, not only the woman's."] }, { heading: "What the evidence supports", paragraphs: ["A few changes are reasonable to consider for fertility and general health alike."], bullets: ["Working gradually toward a healthy weight, in either direction", "Stopping smoking", "Moderating alcohol", "Regular, moderate activity", "Managing conditions such as diabetes or thyroid issues with a doctor"] }, { heading: "What to ignore", paragraphs: ["Extreme diets, miracle fertility teas and drastic detoxes promise far more than they deliver and can do harm. Rapid or severe weight loss is not the goal. Sustainable, moderate change is both kinder and more effective than anything sold as a quick transformation."] }, { heading: "When lifestyle is not the whole answer", paragraphs: ["It is important to say clearly: many people at a healthy weight and living well still face fertility difficulty, and many people who do not fit the ideal conceive without trouble. Lifestyle is one factor among many. If you have been trying without success within the usual timeframes, assessment matters more than another round of self-blame."] }, { heading: "A specialist can put it in proportion", paragraphs: ["A good consultation places lifestyle in context rather than making it the whole conversation. If a change would genuinely help your situation, a specialist can explain why and how. If it would not, they can say so, which is its own relief."] }],
    faq: [{ question: "Does weight affect fertility?", answer: "It can, in either direction, and for both partners, though it is one factor among many. Moderate, sustainable change is more helpful than extreme dieting." }, { question: "Will losing weight guarantee I conceive?", answer: "No. Lifestyle can help but is not a guaranteed fix. Many people at a healthy weight still face difficulty, which is why assessment matters." }, { question: "Are fertility teas and detoxes worth trying?", answer: "Most promise far more than the evidence supports and some can be harmful. Sustainable, moderate lifestyle change is safer and more effective." }],
