    published: "2026-11-24",
    sections: [{ heading: "The run-up is part of the treatment", paragraphs: ["The period before a cycle begins is not empty waiting; it is preparation. Using it well can make the treatment itself feel more manageable and help you go in informed rather than overwhelmed. Preparation is practical, physical and emotional, and none of it needs to be perfect.", "Think of these weeks as getting your footing, not passing an exam."] }, { heading: "Practical preparation", paragraphs: ["A little organisation reduces stress later."], bullets: ["Understand the plan, the steps and the likely timeline", "Get a clear, written breakdown of costs and what may change", "Plan work absences and who can support you", "Gather any records or results your team needs", "Know who to contact with questions between appointments"] }, { heading: "Physical preparation", paragraphs: ["General health changes take time to show, so starting a little early helps. This can include the sensible lifestyle steps that support fertility, taking any recommended supplements such as folic acid where advised, and managing longstanding conditions with your doctor. Ask your team what specifically applies to you rather than following generic checklists."] }, { heading: "Emotional preparation", paragraphs: ["Fertility treatment carries an emotional load as real as the physical one. It helps to decide in advance who you will tell, how you will handle difficult questions, and where you will turn for support. Counselling before treatment, not only during a crisis, can be a genuine strength rather than a sign of weakness."] }, { heading: "Go in with your questions answered", paragraphs: ["Before starting, you should understand what the treatment involves, what it may cost, what the realistic chances are and what happens if things change along the way. A clinic that welcomes these questions in the preparation phase is showing you how it will treat you throughout."] }],
    faq: [{ question: "How far in advance should I prepare for IVF?", answer: "Because general health changes take time to show, starting a few months early can help. Practical and emotional preparation can begin as soon as a plan is agreed." }, { question: "What should I ask before starting treatment?", answer: "Ask about the steps and timeline, a written cost breakdown, your realistic chances, and what happens if the plan changes. A good clinic welcomes these questions." }, { question: "Is emotional preparation really necessary?", answer: "Yes. Treatment carries a real emotional load. Planning support in advance, including counselling, can make the process more manageable." }],
