    published: "2026-12-01",
    sections: [{ heading: "Why this conversation is urgent", paragraphs: ["Some cancer treatments, including certain chemotherapy and radiotherapy, can affect future fertility. Because preservation often needs to happen before cancer treatment begins, timing can be genuinely urgent. Raising the question early, even amid everything else, keeps options open that may close once treatment starts.", "Cancer care rightly comes first. But fertility can be part of the conversation, not an afterthought."] }, { heading: "Options that may exist", paragraphs: ["What is possible depends on age, the type of cancer, the treatment planned and how much time is available."], bullets: ["Egg or embryo freezing for some women", "Sperm freezing for men, which is often quick and simple", "Other approaches a specialist may discuss for specific situations"] }, { heading: "Why to raise it early", paragraphs: ["The single most useful step is to mention fertility to your cancer team as soon as possible, so that a referral to a fertility specialist can happen without delaying essential treatment. Even a brief consultation can clarify whether preservation is possible and appropriate in your case."] }, { heading: "It is a personal decision", paragraphs: ["Not everyone will choose preservation, and that is valid. The goal is simply to make an informed choice rather than to discover later that an option existed and the window passed. A specialist can explain what is realistic for your situation, with no pressure either way."] }, { heading: "Compassion alongside the clinical", paragraphs: ["Facing cancer and thinking about future family building at the same time is a heavy thing to hold. A good team treats both with seriousness and care, and helps you weigh the decision without adding to an already difficult moment."] }],
